Translation patch: libsignal_core::address::ServiceId::parse_from_service_id_string

Change: `input.strip_prefix("PNI:")` becomes a call of the new function `strip_pni_prefix` at the end of the file. It tests that the bytes of `input` start with the bytes `PNI:` (a length check and a comparison of `bytes[..4]` with `b"PNI:"`) and then returns the second half of `input.split_at(4)`. No other line of the file moves.
Why: `str::strip_prefix` is generic over `core::str::pattern::Pattern`, and the extraction excludes `core::str::pattern` (Aeneas does not support its generic associated types). Without this patch Aeneas reports "Could not find: trait_decl_id" at the signature of `strip_prefix` (library/core/src/str/mod.rs, line 2458) and "Could not find: trait_impl_id" at the parser, and leaves the parser without a body.
Behaviour: same result. For a `&str` prefix, `strip_prefix` returns `Some(haystack.get_unchecked(4..))` exactly when `haystack.as_bytes().starts_with(b"PNI:")` (library/core/src/str/pattern.rs, `strip_prefix_of` and `is_prefix_of` for `&str`), and `[u8]::starts_with` is `self.len() >= n && needle == &self[..n]` (library/core/src/slice/mod.rs), the test written here. `split_at(4)` returns `(&input[..4], &input[4..])`; it panics only when 4 is not a character boundary, and here the four bytes before index 4 are ASCII.

diff --git a/rust/core/src/address.rs b/rust/core/src/address.rs
--- a/rust/core/src/address.rs
+++ b/rust/core/src/address.rs
@@ -270,7 +270,7 @@
             Uuid::try_parse(input).ok()
         }
 
-        if let Some(uuid_string) = input.strip_prefix("PNI:") {
+        if let Some(uuid_string) = strip_pni_prefix(input) {
             let uuid = try_parse_hyphenated(uuid_string)?;
             Some(Self::Pni(uuid.into()))
         } else {
@@ -817,3 +817,16 @@
         write!(f, "{}.{}", self.name, self.device_id)
     }
 }
+
+// Modified for Aeneas: `str::strip_prefix` goes through `core::str::pattern`, which the
+// extraction excludes. This is the same byte-prefix test and the same rest of the string.
+fn strip_pni_prefix(input: &str) -> Option<&str> {
+    let bytes = input.as_bytes();
+    let prefix: &[u8] = b"PNI:";
+    if bytes.len() >= prefix.len() && bytes[..prefix.len()] == *prefix {
+        let (_, rest) = input.split_at(prefix.len());
+        Some(rest)
+    } else {
+        None
+    }
+}
